# name: test/sql/bugs/166_in_scope_class_fallback.test
# description: #166 -- :in-scope(.class#X) falls back to the ancestor walk where X is not a scope provider
# group: [bugs]

# THE DEFECT
#
# `:in-scope()` has two argument forms and they disagreed. Measured on
# build/release/duckdb at 3d6b4df, over test/data/sql/example.sql:
#
#   .class#customers                        1
#   .var:in-scope(.class#customers)         0   <- silently empty
#   .var:in-scope(create_table)             4
#   create_table#customers column_definition 4   <- the right answer
#
# The bare-type form walks ancestors by descendant range. The semantic-class
# form reads the precomputed scope struct, which the traversal only populates
# under nodes flagged IS_SCOPE. Measured, SQL's container is a semantic class
# that is not a scope provider:
#
#   create_table       customers  DEFINITION_CLASS     is_scope=false
#   column_definition  id         DEFINITION_VARIABLE  scope.class=NULL
#
# against Python, where the struct is populated:
#
#   class_definition   UserService                     is_scope=true
#   function_definition get_user                       scope.class=367
#
# So `a.scope.class IS NOT NULL` could never be true for SQL, and the form that
# failed is the one the error messages recommend -- the grammar rejects
# `:in-scope(create_table#customers)`, so `.class#name` is the only way to ask.
#
# THE FIX: when the scope struct is empty for this node, fall back to the same
# ancestor walk the bare-type form uses, restricted to semantic class
# definitions and keeping the nearest-enclosing guard. Teague ruled for the
# fallback on 2026-11-05, over flagging SQL's containers as scopes (which would
# change read_ast output for every SQL file and fix only SQL) and over raising
# an error (which refuses the query rather than answering it).
#
# The fallback is strictly additive. It is reachable only where scope.class is
# NULL, so every language with real scope providers keeps the struct fast path
# and its nearest-scope semantics. Section 3 is that control.
#
# NOT FIXED, and no case measured: the function and module forms have the same
# structural hole. A language whose functions are not scope providers would fail
# the same way. No fixture in this repo exhibits it, so it is left alone rather
# than fixed blind.

require sitting_duck

statement ok
LOAD sitting_duck;

# =============================================================================
# 1. THE FIX -- the two argument forms now agree, and on the right number
#
# Asserted as one query so the equality is the assertion, not two numbers that
# happen to match. Two selector calls, which is the cost floor for comparing
# two forms; each costs ~25 s of planning on the v2.0 line (duckdb/duckdb#26036).
# =============================================================================

query II
SELECT
    (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_select('test/data/sql/example.sql', '.var:in-scope(.class#customers)')),
    (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_select('test/data/sql/example.sql', '.var:in-scope(create_table)'));
----
4	4

# =============================================================================
# 2. INDEPENDENT CONTROL -- 4 is the right answer, derived without :in-scope
#
# read_ast only, so this costs no planning. The customers table declares four
# columns, and a descendant selector agrees (measured: 4). If the fixture is
# edited, this fails alongside section 1 instead of letting it drift.
# =============================================================================

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM read_ast('test/data/sql/example.sql') c
WHERE c.type = 'column_definition'
  AND EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM read_ast('test/data/sql/example.sql') t
      WHERE t.type = 'create_table' AND t.name = 'customers'
        AND c.node_id > t.node_id AND c.node_id <= t.node_id + t.descendant_count
  );
----
4

# The container really is a semantic class that is NOT a scope provider. This is
# the precondition the fallback exists for; if SQL ever gains IS_SCOPE on
# create_table, this fails and tells the reader the fallback is now dead code.
query III
SELECT type, semantic_type, is_scope(flags)
FROM read_ast('test/data/sql/example.sql')
WHERE type = 'create_table' AND name = 'customers';
----
create_table	DEFINITION_CLASS	false

# =============================================================================
# 3. CONTROL -- the struct fast path is untouched where it works
#
# Python populates scope.class, so the fallback must not be reached and the
# nearest-scope semantics must not widen to any-ancestor. ast_select_pseudo_
# classes.test pins the same number; it is restated here so a regression names
# #166 rather than the pseudo-class suite.
# =============================================================================

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM ast_select('test/data/python/qualified_names.py', '.fn:in-scope(.class#User)');
----
2
